  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAs the upper closed low pivots and begins its track towards the\nNew England region into Saturday morning, a pocket of colder\n850mb temperatures (around -4 degrees C) along its southern \nperiphery aligns with the WV higher terrain to cause a switch \nover to snow starting around 6am today and continuing into \nSaturday morning. Even still, DGZ snow growth will be lacking\nwhile surface temperatures hover near/above freezing for most\nterrain areas, resulting in SLRs less than 10:1. So most high\nterrain locations will see on and off snow through the day that\nis wet and likely melts in between showers and prevents much (if\nany accumulation).\n\nThe exception is eastern Tucker County where slightly higher\nelevations and stronger upslope favors SLRs more in the 10:1 to\n13:1 range while surface temperatures hover closer to freezing.\nThe result is higher probabilities for accumulations in the 1-3\"\nrange that may stick (even if will be on the wet-side). The\npresence of snow may change heading into Saturday morning as the\nupper low exits, with some guidance suggesting the lose of ice\nnuclei as moisture depths narrows and lends to more freezing\ndrizzle (more likely after midnight tonight). When taking these\nwintry mixes together, add in the prior ice that occurred early\nthis morning and the return of wind gusts in the 40-50 mph range\nas a SW low-level jet comes overhead, localized impacts may \ndevelop so a Winter Weather Advisory was issued.\n\nConfidence is high that impacts cease later Saturday morning\nthanks to height rises and dry southwest flow.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nFor the rest of the region, the passage of the upper closed low\nmeans another round of fairly widespread rain through the\nmorning that transitions to more on and off light rain showers\nthat lingers into Saturday morning (save for the Laurel\nHighlands that see snow mixes but little to no accumulations).\nAdditionally, a stronger southwest low level jet at the base of\nthe low will foster 25-35 mph afternoon gusts near/south of\nPittsburgh with that could touch 40 mph in the higher terrain\n(but near zero probability of reaching Advisory criteria within\nCWA, save far eastern Tucker County).\n\nA last gasp of rain ahead of a mid-level warm front early\nSaturday morning will give way to drier conditions and more\nseasonable temperature for the rest of the day Saturday and\nbrief height rises.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nSouthwest flow resulting in above normal temperature through the\nearly afternoon Sunday is a precursor to the swift approach and\npassage of the next upper trough/surface low pressure system.\nExpect widespread rain spreading west to east through the\nafternoon that generally ends after the cold frontal passage,\nthough lingering lake enhanced showers within residual NW flow\nbehind the trough axis is possible into Monday. Ensemble mean\naccumulations are in the 0.25-0.50\" range with 90th percentile\noutcomes around 1\" suggest minimal flooding concerns with this\nsystem. Delays in the arrival of relatively cold air ensures any\nchange over to snow doesn't occur until Monday at a time when\nQPF wanes, keeping even the \"worst case scenario\" amounts to\nless than 1\".\n\nEnsemble models do show some variance in the timing/structure of\nbroad ridge/trough locations, but even \"Great Lakes trough\"\nsolutions see height rises from Monday into various parts of the\nupcoming week. With limited shortwave movement and height rises,\nthe upper Ohio River Valley is favored to see above normal\ntemperature and a few days of dry weather.",
